-- Passage rg6-evidence-inference-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('99647ec2-d178-53a7-96fa-253da26efcc8', 'rg6-evidence-inference-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Old Ice Harvest$q$, $q$Long before electric refrigerators, families still found ways to keep food cold. In many northern towns during the 1800s, the answer came from frozen lakes and ponds. Each winter, crews cut large blocks of ice and stored them for use in warmer months.

The work began when the ice grew thick enough to hold horses and workers. Crews first scraped away snow so the ice could freeze more evenly. Then they marked the surface in a grid and cut along the lines with long saws. The heavy blocks were floated through open channels of water toward the shore.

On land, workers slid the blocks into tall wooden icehouses. They packed sawdust or straw between the blocks and around the walls. This packing slowed the melting a great deal. A well-built icehouse could keep much of its ice frozen until late summer.

In warm weather, ice wagons carried blocks through town. Homes kept the ice in wooden boxes lined with metal, with food stored on the shelves below. Families placed a card in the window to show how much ice they wanted that day. Children often followed the wagon, hoping for a cool chip of ice on a hot afternoon.

The ice trade grew into a large business. Some ships even carried ice to warm ports far away. By the early 1900s, however, machines that could make ice became more common. Electric refrigerators later arrived in many homes. Within a few decades, the busy winter harvests on frozen lakes had mostly disappeared.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
