-- Passage rg6-evidence-inference-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fdd8d2ba-d0d3-5ea0-af25-191be06d1a27', 'rg6-evidence-inference-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How an Octopus Changes Its Look$q$, $q$An octopus has no shell and no sharp spines to protect it. Even so, it survives in oceans full of hungry fish, seals and sharks. One of its best defenses is its ability to change how it looks in less than a second.

The secret lies in its skin. An octopus has thousands of tiny color sacs just under the surface. Each sac is surrounded by small muscles. When the muscles pull, the sac stretches and shows more color. When they relax, the sac shrinks and the color nearly disappears. By controlling many sacs at once, the octopus can make spots, stripes or patches that match the rocks around it.

Color is only part of the trick. An octopus can also raise small bumps on its skin to copy the rough texture of coral or seaweed. A smooth octopus resting on a bumpy rock would stand out. A bumpy one blends right in.

Scientists find this skill puzzling for one reason. Many studies suggest that octopuses do not see colors the way people do. Yet they still match their surroundings closely. Some researchers think the skin itself may sense light. Others think the octopus judges brightness and patterns rather than exact colors. The question has not been settled.

Octopuses use their color changes for more than hiding. A startled octopus may flash dark colors suddenly, which can confuse an attacker for a moment. That brief pause may give the octopus time to squirt ink and jet away.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
