   $q$A student claims that color changes help an octopus escape, not only hide. Which evidence best supports this claim?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Controlling many sacs at once lets the octopus match the rocks around it.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"supports-different-claim"},{"text":"Some researchers think the octopus skin may be able to sense light.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-supporting-detail"},{"text":"Raising bumps on its skin helps an octopus blend in on rough coral.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"supports-different-claim"},{"text":"A sudden dark flash can confuse an attacker, giving time to squirt ink and leave.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A sudden dark flash can confuse an attacker, giving time to squirt ink and leave.$q$, $q$Paragraph 5 says a dark flash can confuse an attacker so the octopus can "squirt ink and jet away," which is escaping. Matching the rocks is real evidence, but it supports hiding, not escaping.$q$, 'draft', 'fdd8d2ba-d0d3-5ea0-af25-191be06d1a27')
