insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1039d99c-dd05-54d8-a180-d84608de286d', 'rg6-evidence-inference-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Making Paper by Hand$q$, $q$Most paper today comes from huge machines in factories. Yet some artists still make paper one sheet at a time, using a method that is hundreds of years old. The process takes patience, but it lets the maker control the color, thickness and texture of every page.

The first step is to make pulp. Papermakers tear plant fibers or old cotton rags into small pieces and soak them in water. Then they beat the mixture until the fibers separate and float freely. The result looks a bit like thin oatmeal.

Next comes the mold, a wooden frame with a fine screen stretched across it. The papermaker dips the mold into a vat of pulp and lifts it out flat. Water drains through the screen, leaving a thin layer of fibers on top. A gentle shake from side to side helps the fibers lock together. If the papermaker tilts the mold while lifting, the sheet will be thicker on one side.

The wet sheet is then flipped onto a piece of felt. Sheets and felts are stacked and pressed to squeeze out more water. Finally, the sheets are hung or laid out to dry, which can take a day or longer.

Handmade paper often has rough, feathery edges, and no two sheets are exactly alike. Some makers add flower petals or colored threads to the pulp before dipping the mold. Teachers at Brightwater Community Arts Center say students are usually surprised that their first sheets come out uneven. Most improve after a few tries, once they learn to lift the mold slowly and evenly.$q$, 'draft')
