-- Passage rg6-evidence-inference-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('22b09c01-f556-557b-bf69-21e50aea471a', 'rg6-evidence-inference-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Counting the Rain$q$, $q$Weather forecasters use satellites, radar and computer models to track storms. Still, some of the most useful rain records come from ordinary people with plastic tubes in their backyards. Across many regions, volunteers measure rain and snow every morning and share their numbers online.

The equipment is simple. A rain gauge is a clear tube marked with lines, much like a measuring cup. It is placed in an open spot, away from trees and roofs that could block or add water. Each morning at about the same time, the volunteer reads the level, records it and empties the tube.

Why do these backyard numbers matter when radar exists? Radar estimates rainfall from high above, and the estimates can be off. A thunderstorm might drop heavy rain on one neighborhood and barely touch another a few miles away. A network of gauges on the ground shows those differences clearly.

The records are used in many ways. Farmers check them to decide when to water their fields. Scientists compare them with radar to make future estimates more accurate. City workers study them after storms to learn which streets flood most easily.

Volunteer Malik Haddad has measured rain in his yard for six years. He began after a summer storm flooded his street while the official weather station across town recorded only a light shower. He now trains new volunteers at the public library twice a year. He tells them that a missed day is fine, but a guessed number is not, because one wrong number can mislead everyone who uses the data.$q$, 'draft')
