-- Passage rg6-evidence-inference-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d2036755-cbe9-5988-b2da-662e71b2f27d', 'rg6-evidence-inference-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Our Playground Needs Shade Trees$q$, $q$Every spring, our school's playground turns into a griddle. The blacktop soaks up the sun, and by lunchtime the slide is too hot to touch. Students crowd into the thin strip of shade beside the building, and many stop playing altogether. I believe our school should plant a row of shade trees along the edge of the playground.

Shade makes a real difference in temperature. Last May, our science class measured the ground in several spots at noon. The open blacktop read 51 degrees Celsius. The grass under the one old maple near the fence read 29 degrees. That is a difference of more than twenty degrees in the same yard at the same moment.

Trees would also help students stay active. When the playground is too hot, students sit instead of running. Our physical education teacher, Mr. Delgado, has noticed that recess games end early on hot days. More shade would let students play longer and more safely.

Some people worry about cost and care. Young trees do cost money, and they need water during their first few summers. However, our parent group has offered to raise funds, and the eighth graders' service club has volunteered to water the trees. A nearby plant nursery has also offered a discount for schools.

Trees take years to grow tall, so the best time to plant them is now. If we start this fall, students in the younger grades will enjoy real shade before they leave our school. I urge our principal and school leaders to approve the plan at their next meeting.

Priya Raman, Grade 8$q$, 'draft')
