   $q$In paragraph 3, the author writes that the glacier "creeps forward." Why is "creeps" a better choice here than "rushes"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Creeps\" shows that the glacier has stopped moving for good.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-extreme"},{"text":"\"Creeps\" shows that the glacier is moving uphill against gravity.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"\"Creeps\" shows slow, quiet movement, which matches a few centimeters a day.","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"Creeps\" shows that the glacier moves in a scary and dangerous way.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-connotation"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Creeps" shows slow, quiet movement, which matches a few centimeters a day.$q$, $q$The glacier moves "a few centimeters or a few meters each day," so "creeps" fits its slow pace while "rushes" would suggest speed. The text says gravity pulls it downhill, not uphill.$q$, 'draft', '92e5ae9d-69d4-57d8-bd18-4c93d44f09b4')
