-- Passage rg6-figurative-language-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('338e4de6-03eb-59ed-98b0-cf3e3bb871b0', 'rg6-figurative-language-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Blue Cape$q$, $q$The sewing machine in Grandma Ines's spare room was older than Lucia's mother. It was black and heavy, with gold flowers painted along its side. Lucia had always thought of it as a sleeping cat, curled quietly in the corner.

One Saturday, Lucia needed a costume for the school play. She had been cast as a royal messenger, and she needed a cape. "Can we use the machine?" she asked.

Grandma Ines smiled. "That old thing still has a few stories left in it," she said.

She showed Lucia how to thread the needle and guide the fabric. When Grandma pressed the pedal, the machine woke up with a cheerful hum. The needle danced up and down, stitching a neat line across the blue velvet.

Lucia tried next. At first, her seams wandered across the cloth like a lost puppy. She frowned and wanted to quit. Grandma Ines patted her hand. "Rome was not built in a day," she said. "Neither is a cape."

Lucia slowed down. She kept her eyes on the needle and her hands steady. By late afternoon, the seams were straight, and the cape was finished. It was not perfect, but it was hers.

On the night of the play, Lucia swept across the stage in her blue cape. She felt as tall as a tower. Afterward, she hugged her grandmother. "The machine told a new story tonight," she whispered.$q$, 'draft')
