-- Passage rg6-figurative-language-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('21993381-eb45-59f2-a452-331fee5c2025', 'rg6-figurative-language-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Hunters of the Night$q$, $q$When the sun goes down and most birds settle into their nests, the owl's workday begins. Owls are nocturnal hunters, which means they are active at night. Their bodies are built for finding food in the dark.

An owl's eyes are huge compared with the size of its head. They gather every scrap of light, the way a bucket collects raindrops. Owls cannot move their eyes the way people can, so they turn their heads instead. Some owls can twist their necks about three quarters of the way around.

Hearing may be an owl's greatest tool. Many owls have one ear opening set a little higher than the other. This uneven placement helps them pinpoint exactly where a sound is coming from. A barn owl can catch a mouse hidden under leaves or snow using sound alone.

Owls are also known for flying in near silence. Most birds' wings make a whooshing sound. An owl's feathers, however, have soft, fringed edges that muffle the noise. The owl drifts through the night air like a falling leaf.

Because owls eat many mice and other rodents, farmers often welcome them. A single family of barn owls can eat thousands of rodents in a year. To a farmer, an owl is a quiet partner, guarding the fields while everyone sleeps.$q$, 'draft')
