   $q$Why is it effective for the story to follow Arjun's thoughts rather than Nani's?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Readers start out doubtful like Arjun, so they share his growing sense of wonder.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Arjun is the only character who speaks, so readers can only hear from him.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Following Nani would let the author skip the explanation of the shadow.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-purpose"},{"text":"Nani does not know anything about the eclipse, so she could not explain it.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Readers start out doubtful like Arjun, so they share his growing sense of wonder.$q$, $q$Because the story stays with Arjun, readers move with him from "it was always there" to deciding to look "much more carefully." Nani clearly knows a lot, since she explains both the shadow and the red color.$q$, 'draft', 'c9bf87f0-bfa9-5420-a8ad-7258ddba8f0c')
