-- Passage rg6-main-idea-summary-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8510d3c9-cba2-54cb-a6a7-c020dd42b14d', 'rg6-main-idea-summary-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Every Student Should Learn to Type$q$, $q$Students today write essays, research reports and science notes on computers. Yet many of them type with two fingers, hunting for each key. Middle schools should teach keyboarding as a regular class, because strong typing skills help students in almost every subject.

The first reason is speed. A student who types with all ten fingers can often write much faster than one who searches for each letter. When typing is quick, students can spend more of their class time thinking about ideas instead of searching the keyboard.

Typing well also improves the quality of student writing. When fingers keep up with thoughts, students are less likely to lose an idea halfway through a sentence. They also have more time left to reread, revise and fix mistakes before they turn in their work.

Some people argue that students will simply pick up typing on their own by using phones and computers at home. However, habits formed without instruction are often slow and hard to change. A few weeks of practice with a teacher can build correct finger positions that last for years.

Finally, typing is a skill that students will use long after school ends. Many jobs, from nursing to engineering, require people to write reports, emails and records on a keyboard. Learning the skill early saves time for the rest of a person's life.

Schools already teach young students how to hold a pencil. Teaching older students how to use a keyboard is the natural next step. A short daily typing lesson is a small investment that pays off in every class.$q$, 'draft')
