-- Passage rg6-story-details-inference-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('882d3148-0996-576d-af2a-b0ba69346535', 'rg6-story-details-inference-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-story-details-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Fourth Board$q$, $q$Yara Haddad had never played in a chess tournament before. Her school club needed four players for the city team event, and she was the fourth, added the week before because another player had the flu.

Her opponent in the final round was a tall eighth grader named Felix, who wore a club jacket covered in pins. He set up his pieces in four seconds. Yara set hers up slowly, touching each one as if to check that it was real.

Yara's school led two games to one, and only her game was left. If she lost, the teams would be even, and the trophy would go to the other school on tiebreak points. If she won or drew, her school would take it home.

For an hour, they traded pieces until each player had only a king, a rook, and three pawns. Then Felix moved his rook, leaving one of his pawns unguarded. Yara saw it at once. She could take it for free.

She reached for her rook. Then she pulled her hand back and sat on it. Her coach, Mr. Abara, had told the team one rule a hundred times: when you see a great move, look for your opponent's great move first.

She looked. If she took the pawn, Felix's king could march forward and guard his last pawn all the way to the end of the board.

Yara moved her king instead, one quiet square. Felix stared at the board for a long time. Ten minutes later, he offered a draw, and she shook his hand.

Mr. Abara did not cheer. He just tapped the side of his head twice and pointed at her.$q$, 'draft')
