-- Passage rg6-text-structure-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b37c1a79-b7fa-5b09-9a0e-782b3eda6d9d', 'rg6-text-structure-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Fixing the Pickup Line$q$, $q$Every afternoon at 3:15, the road in front of Maple Grove Elementary turned into a parking lot. Cars lined up for blocks, honking and inching forward. Some drivers stopped in the bus lane. Students waited on the sidewalk for twenty minutes or more, and neighbors complained that they could not get out of their driveways.

The school's student council decided to study the problem. For two weeks, council members stood near the entrance with clipboards. They found that most delays happened because each car stopped at the front door while one child searched the crowd for the right ride.

The council proposed a three-part plan. First, each family received a large number card to place on the dashboard. Second, a teacher with a walkie-talkie stood at the end of the line and called out numbers as cars arrived. Third, students waited in numbered zones, so they were ready to walk out the moment their car pulled up.

The plan started on a Monday in October. On the first day, things were a little confusing, and a few cards were missing. By Friday, though, most families had the hang of it.

The results surprised everyone. The average wait dropped from twenty minutes to about seven. The bus lane stayed clear, and the honking nearly stopped. One neighbor, Mr. Okafor, even taped a thank-you note to the school's front door.

Other schools in the district have now asked the council to explain the system. The students learned that careful observation can solve a problem that adults had grumbled about for years.$q$, 'draft')
