-- Passage rg6-text-structure-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('021b59d9-7bf8-54b7-b4b9-1f9691ba9076', 'rg6-text-structure-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Make Your Own Stop-Motion Movie$q$, $q$Stop-motion movies look like magic. Clay figures walk, toy cars race, and paper birds flap their wings, yet nothing in the scene really moves on its own. This guide explains how the trick works and how you can make a short film of your own.

How the Trick Works

A stop-motion film is really a long series of photographs. You take one picture, move your object a tiny amount, and take another. When the pictures are played quickly, about twelve every second, your eyes blend them together. The object seems to move smoothly.

What You Need

You will need a camera or tablet, a stand to hold it still, a few small objects, and a free animation app. A desk lamp helps keep the lighting the same in every shot.

Step by Step

First, plan a very short story, such as a ball rolling off a table. Next, set up your scene and lock your camera in place. Then take a photo, move the object slightly, and take another. Keep going until the action is finished. Finally, use the app to play the photos in order.

Tip Box: Small Moves Matter

If you move your object too far between pictures, it will seem to jump. Try moving it about the width of a pencil each time.

One Last Thought

Ten seconds of film can take more than one hundred photos. That sounds like a lot, but students who try it often say that watching their first movie is worth every click.$q$, 'draft')
