insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('14fcc73f-3c0a-5788-a24e-329e490e74c3', 'rg6-theme-summary-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-theme-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Trolley Map$q$, $q$Mateo had ridden his bike down Linden Avenue nearly every day of his life, and he had never once looked closely at the ground. Then his teacher gave the class a history project. Each student had to find one thing in town that was older than their grandparents.

At the town museum, a volunteer named Mrs. Abernathy unrolled a yellowed map on a glass table. Thin red lines crossed the whole town. "Trolley tracks," she said. "Electric cars ran on them for forty years. People rode them to work, to school and to the lake on Sundays."

Mateo copied the red lines into his notebook. That afternoon he rode slowly down Linden Avenue, staring at the street. Near the post office, where the pavement had cracked, he spotted two strips of rusty metal running side by side. He got off his bike and knelt beside them.

Over the next week, he found more pieces. A curve of rail peeked out beside the bakery. An old brick building on Fifth Street had a stone sign carved with the words "Car Barn." He had always guessed it was a place where people once fixed cars. Now he understood that the trolleys had been kept there at night.

At home, he drew a new map, placing the old trolley routes on top of the streets he knew. When he finished, he noticed that the busiest bus routes in town followed almost the same paths. The town had kept growing along the tracks, even after the trolleys were gone.

On presentation day, Mateo held up his map. "I thought our town was ordinary," he told the class. "Now every street I ride on feels like a story that is still going."$q$, 'draft')
