-- Passage rg6-theme-summary-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5b05a701-5743-55e6-85f7-85d5889a54ef', 'rg6-theme-summary-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-theme-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Measuring Tape$q$, $q$Rafael had been practicing the long jump all spring. His best mark was four meters, and the school record was four meters and twenty centimeters. At the district meet, he wanted that record more than anything.

On his last jump, he sprinted down the runway and launched into the air. He landed hard and fell back slightly, catching himself with one hand behind his heels.

The official, a tall woman with a clipboard, stretched the tape from the board to the front edge of his heel prints. "Four meters, twenty-five," she called out. His teammates cheered and jumped up and down.

Rafael stood up and brushed the sand off his shorts. Then he looked at the pit. Behind his heel prints was a clear dent where his hand had pressed into the sand. He knew the rule: a jump is measured to the mark closest to the board. His hand had touched closer.

His coach, Mr. Lindqvist, was already writing the new record on his clipboard. Rafael's stomach twisted. Nobody else had noticed the handprint.

He walked over to the official. "My hand went back," he said quietly, pointing at the dent. "I think it should be measured from there."

The official looked, nodded and measured again. "Three meters, ninety-eight," she said. "Thank you for telling me."

On the bus ride home, Rafael sat by the window. He had not broken the record. But when Mr. Lindqvist sat down beside him and said, "That was the best thing I saw all day," Rafael found that he was smiling.$q$, 'draft')
