   $q$Why does the author have the team lose the contest even after the oven works?$q$, $q$[{"text":"To show that Joon's idea was not as good as the other teams' ideas.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-purpose"},{"text":"To show that what Amara gained was a new way of working with others, not a prize.","is_correct":true},{"text":"To suggest that science contests are unfair to teams that start late.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"To prove that Amara's first plan with extra foil was the better choice.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$To show that what Amara gained was a new way of working with others, not a prize.$q$, $q$Without a win, the ending focuses on Amara asking "what else was in his notebook," which shows the real change is in her. Joon's idea melted the chocolate when hers did not, so the story does not suggest his idea was weaker.$q$, 'draft', '1c4b0c21-cdcc-54b9-9ab5-b272f28ef127')
