insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ddd97707-5194-5811-90cf-ed551eae73fa', 'rg6-word-parts-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How Seeds Travel$q$, $q$Plants cannot walk, yet their seeds often end up far from the parent plant. Scientists call this spreading of seeds dispersal. It helps young plants find space, sunlight, and water of their own.

Wind is one of the most common ways seeds travel. A dandelion seed has a fluffy top that acts like a tiny parachute. On a breezy day, it can drift across a whole field. Maple seeds have flat wings that spin as they fall, carrying them away from the tree.

Animals transport seeds too. Some seeds have hooks that grab onto fur or socks. A deer might carry a burr for a long distance before it falls off. Birds eat berries and later drop the seeds in new places, often miles away.

Water carries other seeds. A coconut can float on the ocean for weeks and wash up on a distant beach. Its thick shell protects the seed inside from salt water.

A few plants use a surprising method. When their seed pods dry out, the pods burst open and eject the seeds with force. The seeds can land several feet away. People who brush against these pods sometimes jump at the sudden snap.

Each method solves the same problem. A seed that lands right under its parent must compete with that parent for light. A seed that travels has a better chance to grow.$q$, 'draft')
