-- Passage rg6-word-parts-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3e17d36b-ab5d-51df-aaeb-b87570eeb1d4', 'rg6-word-parts-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Digging Into the Past$q$, $q$Archaeologists are scientists who study the objects people left behind long ago. A broken pot, a stone tool, or a single bead can tell them how people lived. Unlike treasure hunters, archaeologists care more about information than about gold.

Before digging, a team maps the site carefully. They divide the ground into squares with string so they can record exactly where each object is found. A piece of pottery in one square might mean something different from the same kind of piece in another.

The work is slow. Team members scrape away soil a little at a time with small trowels and brushes. They sift the loose dirt through screens so that even tiny objects are not lost. Each find is labeled, photographed, and described in a notebook.

Sometimes the most important clues are very hard to spot. Seeds, charcoal, and animal bones can show what people ate and how they cooked. Geologists may join the team to study the layers of rock and soil. The deeper a layer is, the older it usually is.

Back in the laboratory, the team cleans and reconstructs broken objects. Fitting the pieces of a pot back together can take weeks. When the work is done, many objects go on display in a museum.

Archaeology reminds us that ordinary things matter. A cooking pot used every day by one family can become, centuries later, a window into an entire community.$q$, 'draft')
